\title{R1CS}
\author{dhsorens}
\date{2026-08-18}

\p{
    The oldest of the three formats and still the one the [folding](dhsorens-003K) literature is
    written in. It is also the format the [zk.golf](dhsorens-0053) challenges are stated in, which
    makes their published circuits a precise record of what a hash function costs when every
    constraint has to be rank one and lookups are unavailable.
}

\p{
    Questions this page should answer:
    \ul{
        \li{The format: #{(Az) \circ (Bz) = Cz}, the instance and witness split, and what
        "satisfying" means.}
        \li{Sparsity, and why the prover's cost is an MSM or a sparse-polynomial commitment rather
        than an FFT.}
        \li{Relaxed and committed R1CS, the variants folding needs.}
        \li{The cost of a range check or a bit decomposition here, compared with a
        [lookup](dhsorens-002W) in a Plonkish system.}
        \li{zkLean as the R1CS-facing circuit DSL in the verified-zkEVM effort.}
    }
}
